what makes mars attacks more relatable  today than ever before which movies beat  the film at the box office how close did  it come to winning an oscar stay tuned  to find out  as it turns out mars attacks wasn't an  original idea conjured up out of thin  air by tim burton  they may not be as popular as the source  material for other burton projects like  batman or alice in wonderland but the  trading cards that mars attacks is based  on still have a significant pop culture  legacy they were first unleashed into  the world in 1962 and quickly gained  notoriety for depicting aliens attacking  humans in vivid violent detail  decades before the likes of independence  day made it commonplace to destroy  sacrosanct locales and blockbuster  flicks the mars attacks trading cards  were blowing up the golden gate bridge  and setting fire to the white house  parents were outraged but kids were  enamored though these cards weren't  originally around for a very long time  their distinctive visuals and grotesque  humor left a lasting impact on the era's  youngsters they even proved enduring  enough to inspire a big budget movie  decades after they first petrified the  populace  the original mars attacks trading cards  weren't the only ones terrifying  children with horrifying imagery the  dinosaurs attack cards of the 1980s were  just as scary as they depicted dinos  attacking unfortunate humans and each  other these intense illustrations  sparked the imaginations of kids  everywhere and they still inspire plenty  of nostalgia to this day considering  that level of fondness it's no surprise  that a film adaptation of these cards  was once in development there was also a  dinosaur's attack comic series and the  first issue released in 1991 reported  that a film version of the property was  in development  but tim burton ultimately put that  project aside in favor of adapting mars  attacks once jurassic park became a huge  hit a pop culture juggernaut like that  would have made a dinosaur's attack  movie seem like a cheap cash grab  so with that project still as dead as a  t-rex those yearning to see tim burton's  take on dinos will just have to keep on  waiting many of tim burton's films are  explicitly linked to the movies that  inspired him to become a filmmaker in  the first place 1994's ed wood for  example offered him the chance to pay  homage to a famous or infamous b-movie  director mars attacks is no exception  and it even served as another chance for  burton to tip his hat to the works of ed  wood as the director explained to arts  beat la in 1996  i wanted to do something fun with a  bunch of martians with big brains  basically make a modern version of plan  9 from outer space or the war of the  worlds  burton was also of course inspired by  the mars attacks trading cards  particularly how they fused gross out  sci-fi elements with titillating imagery  according to burton this was very much  in the spirit of the 60s kind of sci-fi  movies that i was trying to recreate and  i remember always liking those movies  where there was some sort of weird alien  girl paying tribute to seminal works of  oddball entertainment has worked like  gangbusters for burton over the course  of his career and mars attacks is just  one spectacular example  typically quirky sci-fi movies that pay  tribute to something like trading cards  don't end up with particularly  star-studded rosters but the mars  attacks cast is absolutely packed with  a-list talent some of the famous faces  include jack nicholson glenn close  michael j fox danny devito and even a  cameo appearance from singer tom jones  and that barely even scratches the  surface getting so many big names for  mars attacks was more than just  something done to appease the marketing  department it was a conscious choice on  tim burton's part in order to further  challenge himself as a filmmaker as he  explained to artsbeat la  the only time i had ventured into  bringing together several high-profile  stars was for the batman movies and here  i wanted to repeat this experience on an  even bigger scale there are more than 20  lead roles in mars attacks so it was  quite a challenge for me to put together  this cast luckily this assortment of big  name performers threw themselves into  this uniquely goofy world with gusto  proving their ability to fit right into  burton's imaginative vision  huh calvin autograph anybody got a pen  today every movie from avengers end game  to hello my name is doris gets an  internet-based marketing campaign but  back in 1996 when the mars attacks team  was tasked with building hype internet  marketing for movies was still in its  infancy  it was so much of an event when films  got promotional websites back then that  entertainment weekly ran articles  examining every instance within a  calendar year ew writer bob strauss gave  the mars attack's website a b he offered  praise for how many behind the scenes  details it offered including sketches  from tim burton himself however he  criticized it for an overall lack of  distinction strauss's breakdown of the  site which featured cast bios and links  to relevant sites paints a bare bones  picture  it certainly seemed to have lacked the  kind of audience engagement elements  that are more typical of today's virtual  movie marketing but these were the early  days of online promotion so it's fair to  cut it a little slack movies like mars  attacks deserve our respect for paving  the road for modern marketing without  them we may never have ended up with  viral tick-tock dances  it may sound like a fantasy when  compared to today's cinematic landscape  but there was a time when cgi wasn't  absolutely everywhere as late as the mid  90s this technology was still limited to  only the most major blockbusters  but then slowly but surely those films  began popularizing the idea that digital  wizardry could do just about anything  anyone could imagine  that included realizing weird-looking  alien invaders who keep coming up with  cartoonish ways of dispatching humanity  tim burton has tended to embrace old  school methods of storytelling and  visual effects in many of his projects  but the unique aesthetic of mars attacks  necessitated the use of modern  technology as the director admitted to  artsbeat la i knew we had to create the  aliens with the help of cgi animation  every type of animation has a different  vibe and it's something you can really  analyze and there is something about the  computer medium that seem to work with  these characters because they are all  the same and they have a certain jumping  quality to their movement  computer generated animation did indeed  turn out to be a good fit for the other  worldly characters of mars attacks  allowing it to serve as a shining  example of the 90s cg boom  mars attacks received mixed marks from  critics upon its initial release and the  academy awards have a long-standing  hesitancy towards recognizing science  fiction films when you consider these  two facts together it's awfully apparent  that this film had little hope of  scoring some oscar's love but there was  still the possibility that it could  manage to snag a sliver of golden glory  for a best visual effects nomination  this dream looked like it might become a  reality when in january 1997 variety  reported that mars attacks was indeed  one of seven movies shortlisted for a  nomination in that category alongside  the likes of mission impossible and the  nutty professor  at the time shortlisted titles were  whittled down to three final nominees  who would then compete on oscar night  considering that burton's batman returns  had been nominated for visual effects a  few years earlier these hopes weren't  without precedent so a golden trophy for  mars attacks really did seem possible  within this brief window of optimism  sadly though mars attacks made it no  further in the oscar race  when the official nominees for best  visual effects at the 69th academy  awards were announced independence day  twister and dragon heart made it in  while mars attacks was nowhere to be  found what is wrong with you people  it's hard to imagine a tim burton movie  without an accompanying score from  composer danny elfman over the decades  elfman has translated burton's  distinctive spirit into a wide  assortment of music ranging from the  moodily epic queues of batman movies to  the darkly whimsical tones of charlie  and the chocolate factory for mars  attacks elfman channeled mid 20th  century music to match the retro visual  aesthetic of the film and pay tribute to  its source material even an unfinished  clip from mars attacks proved to be so  vividly imagined that it got elfman's  creative juices flowing it was pretty  much a piece of cake for this  experienced composer to conjure up the  necessary music  as he told collider in 2019 i saw the  opening for mars attacks when tim showed  me just a simple pre-viz version and i  said hold the film i ran out into the  lobby of the theater and i started  making all of these notes to myself for  what became the theme  usually it's much harder than that  with this sudden burst of creativity one  of the most unforgettable parts of mars  attacks was firmly cemented the music  by the time mars attacks hit theaters in  december of 1996 tim burton had  developed a reputation for helming  lucrative fare thanks to the likes of  beetlejuice batman and batman returns  alas mars attacks ended up being a step  backwards in terms of box office prowess  grossing 37.7 million dollars  domestically the film ultimately earned  just half of what beetlejuice amassed  eight years prior it also came in below  what batman and batman returns earned in  just their opening weekend the zany  comedic sensibilities of mars attacks  ended up resonating a little bit more  overseas where the quirky feature  grossed enough to put the worldwide box  office total at 101 million dollars  still the lackluster reception remains  notable considering its pedigree in  front of and behind the camera perhaps  mars attacks was seen as too much of an  independence day clone by the general  public maybe the exposed brains were a  little too creepy or perhaps its trading  card origin was just too niche here's  one thing we do know for certain  december 1996 saw the release of a  couple of mega hit movies in the forms  of jerry maguire and screen clearly more  moviegoers rushed out to see those films  instead of the considerably more  expensive mars attacks burton had  already experienced box office  disappointment a couple of years earlier  with ed wood but this was his first  genuine attempted blockbuster to come up  noticeably short why can't we all just  get along  [Music]  [Applause]  at the time of its release critics  praised mars attacks for its visual  effects and strong performances but they  took umbrage with its emphasis on  stylized dark gags over compelling  characters  all of the anarchic mayhem on display  just wasn't to everyone's tastes these  lukewarm notices made mars attacks a  noticeable step down in terms of  critical reception from prior burden  efforts like beetlejuice but in the  decades since it first hit theaters mars  attacks has managed to endure quite  nicely in the public consciousness  websites like collider and consequence  have published lengthy essays extolling  its virtues with the latter site  praising it for how much it commits to  old-school cinema homages and gruesome  violence  steve dash of slate meanwhile opened  that mars attacks has only grown more  relevant since its 1996 debut thanks to  its depiction of politicians as being  utterly inept in the face of global  catastrophe daesh also praised the film  for committing to the idea that a war  between otherworldly invaders and human  beings would be an incredibly one-sided  affair in favor of the martians the  franchise's legacy can also be seen in  the enduring popularity of the trading  cards with new sets arriving in 2021  mars attacks may not have been  universally beloved upon its initial  release but some of its most outlandish  qualities have aged remarkably well  today it's treasured by the fans who  understand what it's going for and  recognize it as a standout effort in  burton's filmography so i guess like  now we just have to  start over and start rebuilding  everything  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

